<li id="cite_note-125"><span class="mw-cite-backlink"><b><a href="#cite_ref-125">^</a></b></span> <span class="reference-text">Most Italian infantry divisions in North Africa were classed as motor-transportable, with enough vehicles to move artillery and services but not infantry, which could be moved only by vehicles attached to corps and army headquarters and always busy moving supplies. Italian infantry divisions fought as marching units in the Western Desert campaign.</span>
</li>
<li id="cite_note-126"><span class="mw-cite-backlink"><b><a href="#cite_ref-126">^</a></b></span> <span class="reference-text">While a motorised unit, most of its lorries were away hauling supplies and fought the Western Desert campaign as a marching unit, the 7th Bersaglieri Regiment almost permanently detached as a Corps motorised reserve.</span>
</li>
